<td style="border-bottom: 2px solid #2563eb; padding-bottom: 9px; font-size: 11pt; font-weight: bold; color: #1e293b; text-transform: uppercase;">🚨 2. RISK ADVISORY &ndash; VISA-REQUIRED NATIONALITIES</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="padding-top: 15px;">
<div style="background-color: #fff7ed; border: 1px solid #fed7aa; border-left: 4px solid #ea580c; padding: 15px; border-radius: 4px;">
<p style="font-size: 9pt; font-weight: bold; color: #c2410c; text-transform: uppercase; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;">Critical Operational Warning for Seafarers Requiring a Visa:</p>
<ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 18px; font-size: 9pt; color: #7c2d12; line-height: 1.6;">
<li><strong>Seaman's Book &amp; OK-To-Board Limits:</strong> An OK-to-Board Letter and a Seaman's Book do <u>NOT</u> replace a Mexican visa. They do not guarantee airline acceptance or admission into Mexico.</li>
<li><strong>Denial of Entry Experience:</strong> ISS Mexico has experienced real cases where crew members were denied boarding at origin or refused entry by Mexican Immigration despite holding an OK-to-Board letter and seafarer documents.</li>
<li><strong>Chinese Nationals &amp; Visa-Required Groups:</strong> For nationalities requiring a visa (including Chinese, Indian, Pakistani, Russian, etc.), ISS Mexico strongly recommends securing a valid Mexican visa or another qualifying visa (such as a U.S. C1/D1 visa).</li>
<li><strong>Client Written Acknowledgment:</strong> Flights for visa-required crew members must NOT be issued solely on the basis of an OK-to-Board Letter unless the client acknowledges the airline and immigration risks in writing.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</td>
</tr>
